import{_ as n,P as t,d as a,e as o,y as s,q as r,f as c}from"./chunks/framework.D7CAgLCq.js";const b=JSON.parse(`{"title":"D.25 Education, Knowledge Production, the Professions, and Lifelong Learning","description":"","frontmatter":{"title":"D.25 Education, Knowledge Production, the Professions, and Lifelong Learning","xkChars":0,"xkWords":642,"xkLessons":{"D.25":{"title":"D.25 Education, Knowledge Production, Professions, and Lifelong Learning","lead":"What was learned, which qualification was obtained, and which opportunities were gained are different outcomes. Trace corrections to actual downstream decisions and their time windows.","explanation":["Chinese source location: lines 5392–5409, original site location /theory/appendix-d/d-25#section-d-25. See the theory-v3.0 source for the current complete text; the historical research manuscript and excerpts remain available to download and consult in their original edition.","Brief quotation, translated from Chinese: ‘An examination can measure a kind of knowledge without thereby proving fair opportunity.’ Educational aims, accessible learning, actual skills, test interpretation, qualifications, and subsequent opportunities require separate evidence. A test's measurement value cannot close the question of fairness, and unfair opportunity does not imply that nobody genuinely learned anything. [T-main]","Expert instruction can coexist with jointly formulating questions; participation does not require adopting every student suggestion. A reasonable refusal within a curriculum is a specific teaching arrangement, distinct from whether the framework itself is “adopted” under §16.2. Curriculum choices cannot mechanically be treated as the framework's write-back status, nor can non-adoption prove students never participated. [T-main]","Using qualifications for individual decisions requires individual grounds and proportionate review; checks of group distributions alone cannot replace them. Teacher and expert judgments also need accounts of consistency and uncertainty. ‘Human’ is not an exemption from scrutiny. Both model and human judgments must be checked at the unit of their actual use. [T-main]","Real historical reference: the OSR report of March 2, 2021 reviews awarding grades in 2020. Pages 36–37 describe historical school-level attainment inputs and aggregate equality analysis. Pages 50–52 record checks at subject, school, and other levels, checks of certain anomalies, and dual coding, while noting limited individual-level human review before release. This cannot be simplified into an absence of checks. [P-OSR]","Pages 57–58 of the same report discuss appeal arrangements and scope. Page 58 explicitly notes that even with rapid appeals, university-place decisions had already been made using the initial grades. This supports the problem of misalignment between correction and downstream time windows; it does not prove that no corrections reached admissions, nobody regained opportunities, or all losses were irreversible. [P-OSR]","Reissuing grades therefore does not alone prove restoration of original opportunities and all losses. If a qualification correction reaches admissions conditions in time, it can constitute a real downstream improvement, without establishing that a final job or degree place has been obtained. The additional distinction is which node the correction reaches, when it arrives, and which conditions it actually changes. [T-main]","Support from teaching, families, and organizations works through time and resources. Sending a notice, activating an account, parental attendance, administrative attendance records, and growth in knowledge are distinct. Increased participation may also increase translation, care, and maintenance work. Trace who bears that work rather than automatically calling administrative activity improved learning. [T-main]","Ordinary learning is not denied merely because the conditional historical residual required by a historical-increment test is absent. Carrier feedback-mediated learning and an additional historical increment are each tested under their own conditions; the historical increment is not a threshold for all learning. In the source's training example, a person gains new skills but has no local job openings: real learning can coexist with non-employment. [T-main]","Knowledge production should trace actual stages: topic selection, budgets, review, practice, drafting, publication, returns, and production, maintenance, proofreading, translation, and communication. Not every study must be forced into the same workflow. Looking only at submitted papers omits people without resources to produce a draft; reanalysis of the same data does not automatically constitute independent replication. [T-main]","Check professional ability, certification, decision-making powers, and public trust separately. Patients' or practitioners' experience can change the importance of a question and the direction of inquiry, but alone cannot establish population-level causation. Open reading, permission to reuse, contribution, joint topic setting, and recognition of labor are also distinct entry points. Public readability does not mean permission for every reuse. [T-main]","Missing citizen-observation records may indicate poor coverage, not the absence of the local objects concerned. Expression and appeal should not require first learning the framework's terminology. Whether existing skills are recognized differs from whether new training is needed. Displaying credentials, recruitment responses, jobs, wages, and net job creation each have separate outcomes. Cumulative establishments, institutions operating in a given year, formal capacity, and actual headcounts cannot be mixed. Failure to complete a course does not erase partial learning. [T-main]","Connect to §1.8, §6.3, Part XIII, §15.3.3, and §16.16 to examine framework uses, learning conditions, expression of knowledge, analysis and action, and specific educational situations. The eight entry points are: 25.01, educational aims and Dewey and others; 25.02, problem setting in critical pedagogy, including Freire; 25.03, cultural capital, hidden curricula, and qualifications; 25.04, coordination among teachers, students, families, and environments; 25.05, selection stages in universities and knowledge production; 25.06, experts, professionalization, and public knowledge; 25.07, distinct permissions in sharing, citizen science, and openness; and 25.08, lifelong learning, skills, and technological change. This does not claim a complete reading of all the named thinkers' original works. [T-main]"],"argumentSteps":["State educational aims and the units of measured outcomes explicitly.","Separate evidence for notification, participation, attendance, skills, qualifications, and opportunities.","Check individual grounds and proportionate review for individual decisions, not just distributions.","Trace corrections node by node, recording arrival times and actual changes in conditions.","Record those who never reach drafting or observation, and the labor of support, preserving the scope of the account."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Skill formation","leftText":"An actual change in knowledge and ability.","rightTitle":"Obtaining a qualification","rightText":"A record of procedural conferral, not necessarily equivalent to the entirety of one's skills."},{"leftTitle":"Aggregate checks","leftText":"Overall or group-level results.","rightTitle":"Individual review","rightText":"Evidence and anomaly checks for an individual decision."},{"leftTitle":"Reissuing grades","leftText":"Correction of a record.","rightTitle":"Restoration of opportunity","rightText":"Requires timely downstream uptake and actual outcomes."},{"leftTitle":"Reanalysis of the same data","leftText":"May change methods and interpretations.","rightTitle":"Independent replication","rightText":"Independence has additional requirements; the label cannot be assigned automatically."}],"boundaries":["Teaching counterexample from the source: training produces new skills, but there are no corresponding local jobs.","Source boundary: failure to complete a course does not automatically cancel partial learning.","Teaching counterexample: swapping two people's scores within a group leaves the distribution unchanged while changing individual decisions."],"questions":["Can equal means prove individual accuracy?","Does non-employment prove no learning occurred?","Does a correction reaching the relevant conditions in time count as improvement?","Does learning fail to count without a historical increment?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-D-25/figures/d25-results.png","caseLinks":[{"title":"OSR's 2021 review of the models for awarding grades in 2020","href":"https://osr.statisticsauthority.gov.uk/wp-content/uploads/2021/03/Ensuring_statistical_models_command_public_confidence.pdf"}],"caseNotes":[{"title":"OSR's 2021 review of the models for awarding grades in 2020","text":"The report records aggregate equality checks and quality checks at multiple levels while noting limited individual review before release. Page 58 explains that university-place decisions had already been based on the initial grades.","sourceLabel":"Office for Statistics Regulation: Ensuring statistical models command public confidence","sourceUrl":"https://osr.statisticsauthority.gov.uk/wp-content/uploads/2021/03/Ensuring_statistical_models_command_public_confidence.pdf","sourceLocator":"Report dated 2021-03-02: pages 36–37, §3.2.2.3; pages 50–52, §3.3.3.3; pages 57–58, §3.4.2.3. PDF and printed page numbers coincide.","boundary":"This does not claim no checks occurred, all appeals failed, or all opportunities were irrecoverable. Only the quality and timing facts from the specified seven pages are used.","href":"https://osr.statisticsauthority.gov.uk/wp-content/uploads/2021/03/Ensuring_statistical_models_command_public_confidence.pdf"}],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"D.25","start_line":5448,"end_line":5465,"startLine":5448,"endLine":5465}],"outputId":"section-d-25","sourceHash":"9c05fc5b7350d8e94b6456630c46798812214fc5a6b22a6ae7c58536441bb257","teachingExamples":[{"title":"Teaching construction: an unchanged distribution, reversed individual outcomes","text":"Two fictional people, A and B, have true scores of 90 and 50, incorrectly recorded as 50 and 90. The group mean is 70 in both cases, and the distribution remains {50, 90}. Yet with a purely illustrative admissions threshold of 80, their qualification status is reversed. Matching distributions cannot alone validate individual records.","assumptions":"Two artificial people taking the same test; 80 is a fictional threshold and other conditions are omitted.","boundary":"This does not represent real students in 2020 or prove every model wrong."},{"title":"Teaching construction: corrections and windows","text":"In an artificial case, a qualification is corrected on day 3 and admissions are locked on day 5. If received and reviewed on day 4, the correction can change eligibility conditions. If it arrives only on day 6, reopening and remedial procedures require separate examination. Even timely receipt does not remove the need for evidence about available places and the final decision.","assumptions":"Fictional dates; actual admissions are not assumed uniformly impossible to reopen.","boundary":"Timing is not a complete judgment of rights and interests, nor does it imply every late correction is worthless."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Teaching counterexample from the source: training produces new skills, but there are no corresponding local jobs.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]},{"identity":"source_counterexample","text":"Source boundary: failure to complete a course does not automatically cancel partial learning.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Teaching counterexample: swapping two people's scores within a group leaves the distribution unchanged while changing individual decisions.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"Can equal means prove individual accuracy?","answer":"No. Swapping 90 and 50 still leaves a mean of 70.","basis":"The unit of use."},{"question":"Does non-employment prove no learning occurred?","answer":"No. Skills and jobs require separate checks.","basis":"Different outcomes."},{"question":"Does a correction reaching the relevant conditions in time count as improvement?","answer":"It can be a real downstream improvement without amounting to obtaining the final position.","basis":"Retain the local improvement."},{"question":"Does learning fail to count without a historical increment?","answer":"Ordinary carrier-mediated learning is checked under its own conditions, not denied on this basis.","basis":"The model's scope."}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-D-25/figures/d25-results.png","alt":"Separate participation, attendance, skills, qualifications, and subsequent opportunities.","caption":"Based on D.25."},{"path":"/reading-assets/S-D-25/figures/d25-scores.png","alt":"Artificial scores 90/50 are swapped: the mean remains 70, but eligibility at a threshold of 80 reverses.","caption":"Teaching construction."},{"path":"/reading-assets/S-D-25/figures/d25-window.png","alt":"Separate corrected records, downstream receipt and review, and the final position.","caption":"The historical timing issue in OSR's report and an artificial timing construction."}],"diagramBrief":"Three figures distinguish learning outcomes, individual and aggregate checks, and correction windows.","applicability":{"realCase":"applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"This unit concerns methodological judgment. Actual sources support only the listed facts; the diagram and teaching construction each retain their own status."},"openQuestions":["Each student's appeals, final admission, and losses were not tracked.","This does not claim comprehensive research into educational philosophy or all open-science practices."],"taskId":"S-D-25","sources":[{"id":"T-main","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§D.25, historical Chinese research manuscript lines 4917–4934","claim_support":["Distinct educational outcomes, individual review, ordinary learning, boundaries of knowledge production, and eight entry points."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v3.0.1."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"P-OSR","title":"Office for Statistics Regulation: Ensuring statistical models command public confidence","url":"https://osr.statisticsauthority.gov.uk/wp-content/uploads/2021/03/Ensuring_statistical_models_command_public_confidence.pdf","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"2021-03-02","locator":"Report dated 2021-03-02: pages 36–37, §3.2.2.3; pages 50–52, §3.3.3.3; pages 57–58, §3.4.2.3. PDF and printed page numbers coincide.","claim_support":["The 2020 grading models used historical school-level attainment. Aggregate equality checks differ from individual review. Some quality checks occurred, but individual human review before release was limited. Even with rapid appeals, university-place decisions had already been made using the initial grades."],"limits":["The lesson author read the specified seven pages in full, without claiming to have read all 76 pages. The account does not claim no human checks, universal correction failure, or any particular quantity of irreversible loss."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"theory-v3.0","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v3.0, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v3.0 / 2026-11-21","locator":"D.25　教育、知识生产、专业与终身学习; Chinese source lines 5392–5409; SHA-256 0d9e445bcba84380a572d248dad4836013b735d7d33589f29ea0c6f1aeb61c10","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v3.0; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-D-25/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-D-25/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-D-25/source/S-D-25.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-D-25/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text, section locations and lesson wording correspond to v3.0; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"6ec20cc198bb8b57954ae014773bb50132c6f9f702cf543dfa7a971ceb7bc2d0","source_sha256":"0d9e445bcba84380a572d248dad4836013b735d7d33589f29ea0c6f1aeb61c10"}}}},"headers":[],"relativePath":"en/theory/appendix-d/d-25.md","filePath":"en/theory/appendix-d/d-25.md"}`),d={name:"en/theory/appendix-d/d-25.md"};function l(u,e,p,h,g,m){const i=t("SectionLesson");return a(),o("div",null,[e[0]||(e[0]=s("",1)),r(i,{section:"D.25"}),e[1]||(e[1]=c("div",{class:"theory-source"},null,-1))])}const y=n(d,[["render",l]]);export{b as __pageData,y as default};
